            <ArticleTitle>Current perspectives in vulvodynia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>423-36</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/whe.09.30</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Vulvodynia, or chronic vulvar pain, is a common but poorly understood condition. Although its etiology is not well understood, it appears to be multifactorial. As such, treatment options are targeted to reduce singular symptoms in a piecemeal fashion. A number of randomized, controlled trials have been conducted and at least one paper on combination therapy has been published; however, further systematic research is needed in order to more fully inform clinical practice.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
